#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199269} :activity/id #uuid "9c0cd1dd-d41d-511e-aa18-44cf1df53bbc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049639 :topic/id #uuid "077676ad-6f2b-5e36-b6e9-f37bfd844a79" :topic/math-academy-id 4151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199461 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\\mathbf T: (u,v,w) \\longrightarrow \\left(x(u,v,w), \\, y(u,v,w), \\, z(u,v,w) \\right)$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^3,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = R,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7875753545cba8babd08fccaf55236c6b32bfc56f43f496343174acc198f1d9.png)\n\nTo compute the integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R,$ we use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right| \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for double integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "a43397ab-8a32-5928-ba64-90ad84532ab1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c8fb7bf0-e270-5a21-96df-05f4bb90a104" :step/math-academy-id 42964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198039 :question/id #uuid "d123b7fd-5a13-50c0-aa8c-dc196f4b1bcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a898f345-9210-5976-9a42-f415a0e7eaf5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186